From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 22:54:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10335/11362] Restore the hourly progress dashboard as a script
 (REQ-INFRA-6840)

The hourly outer-loop status report drifted from a compact scannable block into
paragraphs. The operator noticed and asked for the block back, which is a fair
complaint: two prose reports cannot be compared at a glance, and the drift is
one-directional because prose is easier to write than a table is to maintain.

A script fixes it permanently -- same fields, same order, every hour -- and closes
two error classes this session hit repeatedly:

  * Elapsed time is COMPUTED, never estimated. An agent has no internal clock and
    reliably reports days-old events as months old; the operator called this
    "disconcerting" and they were right. `days_since` returns None on an
    unparseable date rather than guessing, because a wrong age is worse than an
    absent one -- the wrong one gets repeated as fact.
  * Process liveness is READ from /proc, and outcome counts are COUNTED from
    ops/conductor-log.md. Both were recalled before, and a remembered outcome mix
    is how a bad day gets reported as a good one.

It also surfaces the state that is easiest to lose track of: flags that are
shipped but `unevaluated` in the ledger. Right now that is 4 of 4 -- work that
exists and cannot pay off until something measures it.

And it reports a dead job's death receipt (REQ-INFRA-6830) when one exists, or
says NO receipt explicitly when it does not, because the absence is itself
evidence of SIGKILL, OOM, or a kernel event. A blank there is what left the
2026-08-29 A/B death unexplained.

Read-only by construction: opens files, asks the OS about processes, writes
nothing.

8 tests, 5/5 mutations RED (unparseable date guessing 0, liveness always true,
missing receipt shown as a blank, outcome counts ignoring the day filter, header
dropping the date). Restores byte-identical.

